    "result_text": "All green. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## What changed\n\nThe `householdId` is now **deterministic** \u2014 the first 12 hex characters of `SHA-256(normalizedLastName + '|' + postcode)` \u2014 so owners sharing a lastName and postcode share it automatically, and the household is keyed on `(lastName, postcode)`.\n\n### Code (`.../rest/function/owner/`)\n\n1. **`AssignHousehold.java`** \u2014 rewritten. It no longer reads `sharesHousehold`, scans for existing members, or back-links other owners. It just computes and sets the deterministic id from the owner's own normalized lastName + postcode. Every owner now always carries a householdId.\n\n2. **`CheckOwnerIdentityUnique.java`** \u2014 now a **household duplicate check**. It rejects with 409 (`OwnerIdentityConflictException`) when an existing owner shares the new owner's `householdId` (i.e. same lastName + postcode). `sharesHousehold: true` bypasses the block \u2014 the owner is created as a declared household member. (Previously it matched the whole `identityKey` including telephone, so same-lastName+postcode owners with different phones slipped through.)\n\n3. **`AssignPossibleDuplicate.java`** \u2014 a declared member (`sharesHousehold: true`) is now left `possibleDuplicate = false` and short-circuits before the soft-match scan: \"a declared member is not a suspected duplicate.\"\n\n4. **`AssignHouseholdSize.java`** \u2014 logic unchanged (it already counts by `getHouseholdId()`); now that the id is always populated, the household-size input to the membership pipeline keys off the computed household. Javadoc updated.\n\n### Docs\n\n`openapi.yml` descriptions for `sharesHousehold`, `householdId`, `identityKey`, and `possibleDuplicate` updated to describe the deterministic, `(lastName, postcode)`-keyed behavior.\n\n### Tests\n\nThe full suite (the `AcceptanceTest` \u2014 `coreSameLastNameAndPostcodeShareHouseholdId`, verifying same lastName+postcode \u2192 same householdId, different lastName \u2192 different id, and that `sharesHousehold` lets the second same-household owner through) passes: **Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0 \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.**\n\nOne behavior note worth flagging: because the 409 gate now fires on `(lastName, postcode)` before the soft-match scan, the only same-household owners that reach `AssignPossibleDuplicate` are declared members (which are explicitly not flagged) \u2014 so with this model `possibleDuplicate` effectively never becomes `true`. That follows directly from the spec's rule that a non-declared same-household owner is rejected outright.",
